# `std.json` — typed JSON in/out

Client-side JSON for `.ssc` UI code: decode a fetch result into a **navigable
`JsonValue`** and build request bodies from **structured builders** — no more
hand-rolled substring decoders or duplicated escape helpers.

Navigation is **total**: a missing key, wrong shape, or parse failure funnels to a
Null `JsonValue` / zero-default, never a crash. Use `opt*` / `jsonRead` when
absence must be distinguished. Money is exact: read amounts with `asDecimal`
(lossless), never `asDouble`. See [`specs/std-ui-typed-json.md`](../../specs/std-ui-typed-json.md).

[JsonCoreErr, jsonCoreParseStrict, jsonCoreParseTolerant, jsonCoreRender](json-core.ssc)
[Either, Left, Right](either.ssc)

```scalascript
opaque type JsonValue = Any

// Native/provider boundary only: parsing and rendering stay in json-core.ssc.
// The bridge wraps portable JsonCore ADTs for method navigation and converts
// ordinary runtime values into those ADTs before the pure renderer sees them.
extern def __jsonCoreInstallRenderer(render: Any => String): Unit
extern def __jsonCoreWrap(value: Any): JsonValue
extern def __jsonCoreWrapStrict(result: Any): JsonValue
extern def __jsonCoreRawStrict(result: Any): Any
extern def __jsonCoreEncodeValue(value: Any): Any

val __jsonCoreRendererInstalled = __jsonCoreInstallRenderer(jsonCoreRender)

// ── Parse → navigable JsonValue ─────────────────────────────────────────────
/** Tolerant parse — malformed input yields a Null JsonValue (never throws).
 *  The result navigates totally:
 *    jsonValue(body).get("amount").asDecimal       // exact money, lossless
 *    jsonValue(body).get("steps").asList.map(...)  // [] if absent
 *    jsonValue(body).getOrElse("country", "??")    // default on miss
 *  Accessors on the result (members of the JsonValue value):
 *    get(key) / at(idx) / isNull
 *    asString / asInt / asDouble / asBool / asList / asDecimal
 *    optString / optInt / optDecimal / getOrElse(key, fallback) / raw */
def jsonValue(s: String): JsonValue =
  __jsonCoreWrap(jsonCoreParseTolerant(s))

/** Strict parse into ordinary ScalaScript values. */
def jsonParse(s: String): Any =
  __jsonCoreRawStrict(jsonCoreParseStrict(s))

/** Parse, and say WHY when it fails, instead of aborting the thread.
 *
 *  Reported from rozum (`json-parse-has-no-fallible-spelling`): a server handling
 *  input it did not write could not refuse it. `jsonParse` aborts, and `jsonValue` is total but
 *  cannot DISCRIMINATE — `""`, `"not json"`, a truncated object and the literal `null` all answer
 *  `isNull`, so "was this text JSON at all?" had no spelling on any lane. Their workaround was to
 *  inspect the first byte, which cannot be right in general.
 *
 *  `Left` carries the parser's own message and offset, so a handler can answer 400 with something
 *  the caller can act on rather than a bare "bad request".
 *
 *  BUILT ON `__jsonParseError` RATHER THAN ON A NEW EXTERN, and that is the whole design: the error
 *  text comes from one place, the success value comes from `jsonParse` which every lane already
 *  implements, and there is nothing new for four backends to get subtly different. */
def jsonParseEither(s: String): Either[String, Any] =
  val err = __jsonParseError(s)
  if err == "" then Right(jsonParse(s)) else Left(err)

/** The parse error for `s`, or `""` when it parses. ONE def, and the reason it is a def rather than
 *  an `extern` is that json-core already knows the answer on every lane that runs json-core.
 *
 *  On the rust lane it is replaced by an INTRINSIC, exactly as `jsonParse` is — that lane cannot
 *  lower `std/json-core.ssc` at all today (`rust-lane-refuses-the-tolerant-json-parse-while-the-
 *  panicking-one-builds`), so a def written on json-core would be refused there. Because the
 *  intrinsic replaces the def, this body is never walked on rust and the feature works regardless of
 *  that entry. */
def __jsonParseError(s: String): String =
  jsonCoreParseStrict(s) match
    case JsonCoreErr(message, offset) => "invalid JSON at " + offset + ": " + message
    case _                            => ""

/** Strict parse into the total navigable wrapper. */
def jsonRead(s: String): JsonValue =
  __jsonCoreWrapStrict(jsonCoreParseStrict(s))

/** Serialize a JsonValue (or any value) to a compact JSON string. */
def jsonStringify(v: Any): String =
  jsonCoreRender(__jsonCoreEncodeValue(v))

/** Legacy total/optional lookup helpers over parsed raw values. */
extern def lookup(value: Any, key: Any): Any
extern def lookupOpt(value: Any, key: Any): Option[Any]

// ── Structured builders for request bodies ──────────────────────────────────
// Runtime-escaped; build bodies as values, never by string concatenation. Each
// builder yields the JSON text of its node, joined by jObj/jArr.

def jsonEscape(s: String): String =
  val encoded = jsonStringify(s)
  encoded.substring(1, encoded.length - 1)

/** Encode a string scalar (quoted + escaped). */
def jStr(s: String): String   = jsonStringify(s)
/** Encode a pre-rendered numeric token verbatim (caller passes textual form). */
def jNum(s: String): String   = s
def jBool(b: Boolean): String = if b then "true" else "false"
/** Lossless exact-decimal token — emits the Decimal's plain text (no Double). */
def jDecimal(d: Decimal): String = jsonStringify(d)
/** Pair a key with an already-encoded value (jStr/jNum/jObj/jArr/...). */
def jField(key: String, value: String): String = jStr(key) + ":" + value
def jObj(fields: List[String]): String = "{" + fields.mkString(",") + "}"
def jArr(items: List[String]): String  = "[" + items.mkString(",") + "]"
```
